<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <PackageId>Barbatos.SecsGem.Objects</PackageId>
    <TargetFrameworks>$(BarbatosTargetFrameworks)</TargetFrameworks>
    <RootNamespace>Barbatos.SecsGem.Objects</RootNamespace>
    <PackageTags>secs;e39;e5;stream14;stream2;objectservices;getattr;setattr;attributes;namelist;objspec</PackageTags>
    <Description>Object services (SEMI E39) for .NET, across both the streams it uses. Stream 14: GetAttr and SetAttr for one object or many, the three namelists that say which object types exist, which instances of a type exist and which attributes an object has, and the actionable half - create, delete, act. Either end may ask and either may answer, so one class does both. The enhanced half of stream 2 (S2F49-S2F64): the same questions GEM asks a machine, addressed to one object instead - a command, a report binding, equipment constants, status variables, data variables, alarms and diagnostics, each with an OBJSPEC in front, because a cluster tool has four chambers and S1F3 has nowhere to put the question which one. Both halves are a protocol over data other standards define - E87 carriers, E90 substrates, E94 control jobs, GEM variables - so this package supplies the protocol and your application supplies the data, through one interface per half. Depends on Barbatos.SecsGem.Hsms, not on GEM.</Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Barbatos.SecsGem.Core\Barbatos.SecsGem.Core.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Barbatos.SecsGem.Hsms\Barbatos.SecsGem.Hsms.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
